This task was already finished when I picked it up, so I changed nothing. The previous attempt committed it as e2bb4807 with a full commit body. I checked the committed state: cargo fmt --check and cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings are clean, cargo test passes 7,648 tests with 0 failures, and nothing is left uncommitted.

What the commit does:
• Mentions now pass. The text of a commit or issue message is treated as data, not as a command, so git commit -m "kill -9 1 at $(date)" is no longer refused. This covers git commit -m and --message in all their spellings (including -am, and after git -C dir or git -c k=v), and --body/-b/--title/-t on gh issue, gh pr and gh release. It reuses the existing echo/printf mention path in bash_parse.rs rather than adding a new classifier.
• One deviation from the plan. The plan said any gh subcommand; only issue, pr and release are covered. A gh extension or shell alias could pass a -b value to a shell, so those stay refused.
• What still gets refused. Anything inside $( ) or backticks within the message is still checked. So is a command chained after it with &&, | sh, git commit -F file, git merge -m, sudo git, /usr/bin/git, gh api -f body=, and python3 -c / bash -c / node -e. Each of these 15 rows was checked as refused before the change.
• Recall control. Disabling the new branch made the must-pass test fail by name; restoring it made the tests green again.
• Old vs new comparison. I compared verdicts on 33 trigger phrases across every argument shape. Every "now passes" change is inside a message value, none are outside it, and none go from passing to refused. The table was posted on #1210 before the commit.
• What the built binary shows. In -p mode the old and new binaries both run the example without a warning, because that path never asks for confirmation. The fix is only visible at the REPL confirmation prompt and when a sub-agent's command is refused, which is what the unit-level probe checks.

Not fixed here: two cases are filed as their own issues, both open, and the docs page (troubleshooting/safety.md) now points to them instead of #1210:
• #1222: a dangerous phrase inside a python3 -c string is still refused, because yoyo can't tell a Python string from os.system(). This is the case that refused a command earlier today.
• #1223: a separate hard block, which --yes can't override, still stops git commit -m "... rm -rf / at $(date)" and echo with the same text whenever the command contains a $( ).

